  <section id="about">
    <h2>About</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      I have always seen technology as a tool, not the goal itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      My foundation is management. That is where I learned to read problems,
      processes, targets, and how people actually work on the ground. The
      engineering side I built up slowly, through a route I chose myself:
      bootcamps, practice, and work that kept asking for more.
    </p>
    <p>
      It started simply. In 2021 I began looking after a foundation's database
      with MySQL, phpMyAdmin, and XAMPP. From then on a database was no longer
      coursework, but something to be kept safe because other people relied on
      it.
    </p>
    <p>
      After that I took several bootcamps through 2022 and 2023. HTML, Laravel,
      JavaScript, React, Next.js, Express, MongoDB, and React Native. I learned
      the technical side not to collect a stack, but because there were problems
      I wanted to solve.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 2023 the two sides began to meet. I won a national Marketing Plan
      Competition at Universitas Andalas, then also won a Qur'an application
      design competition and reached its national finals. One from the business
      side, one from the technology side.
    </p>
    <p>
      At Telkomsel I started in customer service. I ran Telkomsel and Telkom
      programmes whose rules were fairly complex, then translated them into
      conversations customers could follow. Two years in that seat gave me a
      perspective that is hard to get from documentation: how a programme really
      runs on the ground.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now I build the systems that support that work. Systems that turn raw
      collections data into daily operational decisions, from ingestion and the
      database through to the dashboard the team uses.
    </p>
    <p>
      What I care about most is not a dashboard that looks busy. What matters is
      a correct number. Because one metric with the wrong definition can make
      the decisions wrong too, without anyone noticing.
    </p>
  </section>

